<p>SQL describes the rows you want from related facts, or the rows you intend to change. A query can run without error and still answer the wrong question: a customer repeated once per order, a total doubled by a join, or a missing balance silently treated as zero.</p>
<p>We will build a shop’s January follow-up list: open orders with money still due, including orders whose value is not yet known. The same small database will carry us from a first SELECT to a complete report, a paged list and a checked change. At each step, we will say what one result row means and calculate enough of the answer by hand to recognise a mistake.</p>
<p>The runnable syntax is <strong>PostgreSQL 18</strong>, verified on native PostgreSQL 18.6. SQL’s selection, joining and grouping ideas carry across relational engines; client commands, type names and some clauses differ. PostgreSQL-specific spelling is identified where it first matters. This follows <a href="/review/relational-next/">Relational databases</a>; the deeper articles linked along the way take up the cases this first working route introduces.</p>

