<section aria-labelledby="start-with-a-database-you-can-inspect-and-reset">
 <h2 id="start-with-a-database-you-can-inspect-and-reset">Start with a database you can inspect and reset</h2>
 <p>Use a fresh disposable database. The setup below drops and recreates four ordinary tables, so it belongs in that exercise database alone. You need a running PostgreSQL 18 server, its <code>createdb</code> and <code>psql</code> client programs, and a database role allowed to create the exercise database. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/download/">official packages and installers</a> supply the platform-specific installation instructions; select PostgreSQL 18 and include the command-line tools.</p>
 <details><summary>Start a private practice server on macOS or Linux</summary>
  <p>After installing the server tools, open a fresh terminal as your ordinary OS user. Set <code>SQL_PRACTICE_BIN</code> to that installation’s <code>bin</code> directory. Both version checks below should report 18. The new server uses a private local socket, accepts your OS identity and has no TCP listener. It leaves existing servers alone.</p>
  <pre data-private-practice-start><code is:raw>SQL_PRACTICE_BIN=/path/to/postgresql-18/bin
export PATH="$SQL_PRACTICE_BIN:$PATH"
postgres --version
psql --version
SQL_PRACTICE_DIR=$(mktemp -d /tmp/sql-practice.XXXXXX)
chmod 700 "$SQL_PRACTICE_DIR"
unset PGHOSTADDR PGSERVICE PGSERVICEFILE PGOPTIONS
export PGHOST="$SQL_PRACTICE_DIR" PGPORT=5432 PGUSER="$(id -un)"
initdb -D "$SQL_PRACTICE_DIR/data" --locale=C --encoding=UTF8 \
  --auth-local=peer --auth-host=reject
pg_ctl -D "$SQL_PRACTICE_DIR/data" -l "$SQL_PRACTICE_DIR/server.log" \
  -o "-h '' -k '$SQL_PRACTICE_DIR' -p 5432" -w -t 15 start</code></pre>
  <p>Continue in this terminal: the exported host, port and role direct the client commands to this server. If startup fails, read its printed error and <code>server.log</code> before continuing. Keep the directory name for the cleanup at the end of the exercise.</p>
 </details>
 <p>On Windows, follow the installer’s server-start instructions and use its practice connection details. If using an existing lab server on any platform, pass the same host (<code>-h</code>), port (<code>-p</code>) and role (<code>-U</code>) to both commands below. <code>createdb</code> creates a database on that running server; <code>psql</code> connects a terminal to it. They do not start the server.</p>
 <pre><code>{`createdb sql_working
psql --no-psqlrc -d sql_working`}</code></pre>
 <p>At the psql prompt, run <code>SELECT version(), current_database(), current_user;</code>. Expect an 18.x server, database <code>sql_working</code> and your practice role. A missing command means the client tools or their search path need attention; a connection error names the address or socket the client tried. Check those details against the intended server before loading the fixture.</p>
 <p>Save the setup as <code>setup.sql</code> in your working directory. Inside psql, <code>\i setup.sql</code> reads the file; repeating it resets the exercise. <code>\dt</code> lists tables, <code>\d orders</code> shows columns, types, the primary key, checks and foreign keys, and <code>\q</code> exits. The backslash commands belong to psql, the client; they are not SQL statements to send through an application driver. psql accepts a statement ending in a semicolon, including one spread over several lines.</p>
 <details><summary>Complete setup and reset script</summary><Sql code={setup} label="setup.sql: the complete fixture, in a disposable database" /></details>
 <p>A customer has orders; an order has lines and payments. Each payment here belongs to an order, so it can settle that order. Amounts are invented values in a single currency, with no refunds, taxes or currency conversion. Lines store their extended amounts already: there is no quantity multiplication to infer.</p>
 <table><thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>One stored row</th><th>Identity</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>customers</td><td>One customer</td><td>customer_id</td></tr>
  <tr><td>orders</td><td>One placed order</td><td>order_id; customer_id refers to customers</td></tr>
  <tr><td>order_lines</td><td>One line within an order</td><td>(order_id, line_no)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>payments</td><td>One receipt for an order</td><td>payment_id; order_id refers to orders</td></tr>
 </tbody></table>
 <p>Check where you connected, then look at the rows. <code>version()</code> identifies the server actually answering, which may differ from the installed client version. In psql, <code>\pset null 'NULL'</code> makes missing values visible in the output rather than blank.</p>
 <Sql code={inspect} label="Identify the server and inspect an explicitly ordered sample" />
 <p>Try <code>SELECT order_idd FROM orders;</code>. PostgreSQL reports that column <code>order_idd</code> does not exist and points at the reference. Read the first error, locate the named table, column or constraint, and compare it with the inspected definition. The prompt still accepts a corrected query. If you make an error inside an explicit transaction, PostgreSQL leaves that transaction failed; issue <code>ROLLBACK;</code> before continuing. The later change exercise uses an explicit transaction deliberately.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="decide-what-one-result-row-means">
 <h2 id="decide-what-one-result-row-means">Decide what one result row means</h2>
 <p>“Outstanding orders” leaves several decisions open. Does a partially paid order belong? What if no lines have arrived? Is January about placement or payment? Write a <strong>result contract</strong> before choosing clauses:</p>
 <ul>
  <li>One row represents one order, identified by order_id. Return its customer’s name, exact numeric amount still due and a Boolean saying whether any payment exists.</li>
  <li>Include open orders placed from 1 January 2026 at 00:00 UTC up to, but excluding, 1 February at 00:00 UTC. Use all currently recorded payments for those orders. This is a current follow-up list for January placements, not a balance as it stood on 31 January.</li>
  <li>Subtract receipts from the line total. No receipts means zero paid. No lines means the order is unvalued, so its balance is NULL and it still needs attention. Exclude known zero or negative balances and cancelled orders.</li>
  <li>Keep distinct order identities even when every displayed value matches. Display oldest placement first, breaking equal timestamps by order_id.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>The compact fixture is below. Timestamps are UTC; dates are in 2026.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="All orders in the SQL fixture"><table><thead><tr><th>Order</th><th>Customer</th><th>Placement</th><th>Status</th><th>Lines</th><th>Receipts</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>100</td><td>Mina</td><td>1 Jan, 00:00</td><td>open</td><td>10</td><td>10</td></tr>
  <tr><td>101</td><td>Mina</td><td>10 Jan, 09:00</td><td>open</td><td>20 + 10</td><td>10 + 5</td></tr>
  <tr><td>102</td><td>Mina</td><td>10 Jan, 09:00</td><td>open</td><td>20</td><td>None</td></tr>
  <tr><td>103</td><td>Tariq</td><td>31 Jan, 23:59:59</td><td>open</td><td>40</td><td>40</td></tr>
  <tr><td>104</td><td>Tariq</td><td>1 Feb, 00:00</td><td>open</td><td>50</td><td>None</td></tr>
  <tr><td>105</td><td>Edda</td><td>20 Jan, 12:00</td><td>cancelled</td><td>60</td><td>None</td></tr>
  <tr><td>106</td><td>Edda</td><td>22 Jan, 12:00</td><td>open</td><td>None</td><td>None</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
 <p>Order 101 owes 30 − 15 = 15 and has a payment; 102 owes 20 and has none; 106 has an unknown balance and has none. Those are our three expected rows, in that order. Orders 100 and 103 are settled, 104 is outside the period, and 105 is cancelled. Keeping those excluded identities in the fixture lets us check the boundary as well as the answer.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="select-rows-and-calculate-their-columns">
 <h2 id="select-rows-and-calculate-their-columns">Select rows and calculate their columns</h2>
 <p>A <code>SELECT</code> names the output expressions. <code>FROM</code> supplies their input rows; <code>WHERE</code> keeps qualifying rows. Start with the open January orders, before calculating their balances:</p>
 <Sql code={candidates} label="The five candidate orders, before checking money due" />
 <p>This returns 100, 101, 102, 106 and 103 in placement order. <code>AS shipping</code> gives the calculated column its output name. <code>CASE</code> selects the first branch whose condition is true, otherwise its ELSE value: only 102 is “sent”. Explicit columns keep both the question and the result shape visible; <code>SELECT *</code> is convenient for exploration but changes its output when a table gains columns.</p>
 <p>Read the query as a logical transformation: take orders, retain open January rows, calculate the selected columns, then sort. That is a way to reason about the answer. The engine may find and filter the rows by a different physical route.</p>
 <p>Conditions combine with <code>AND</code>, <code>OR</code> and <code>NOT</code>. AND binds more tightly than OR: <code>status = 'open' AND customer_id = 1 OR customer_id = 3</code> includes Edda’s cancelled order. To require open orders from either customer, write <code>status = 'open' AND (customer_id = 1 OR customer_id = 3)</code>. Use parentheses to state the intended grouping whenever conditions mix.</p>
 <p>Repeating a value is not a duplicate entity. <code>SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_id IN (101, 102)</code> returns two rows containing 1. <code>SELECT DISTINCT customer_id</code> returns one, suitable for a list of customer IDs. DISTINCT compares the entire selected row, so adding order_id makes these rows different again. Use it when the contract asks for different values; it cannot generally repair a query written at the wrong level of detail.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="make-values-missing-facts-and-time-explicit">
 <h2 id="make-values-missing-facts-and-time-explicit">Make values, missing facts and time explicit</h2>
 <p><code>NULL</code> marks a missing value. Our shipped_at column is nullable because an order may not have shipped. Comparing a missing timestamp with a timestamp cannot establish equality or inequality; the result is <strong>unknown</strong>, represented by a null Boolean. Use <code>IS NULL</code> to test for missingness.</p>
 <Sql code={values} label="A missing-value test and a type-sensitive calculation" />
 <p>Both wrong_test values are unknown, including the row whose shipped_at is missing. A WHERE condition keeps only true rows, rejecting false and unknown alike. <code>NOT (shipped_at = NULL)</code> stays unknown; it does not find the shipped orders. For compound conditions, false AND unknown is false, true AND unknown is unknown, true OR unknown is true, and false OR unknown is unknown. This explains why a missing fact can disappear from a seemingly reasonable filter.</p>
 <p>Types change expressions too. PostgreSQL divides two integers as integers, so <code>5 / 2</code> returns 2. Its <code>::numeric</code> spelling casts the first operand to an exact decimal type; the more portable spelling is <code>CAST(5 AS numeric)</code>. Casting after the division would preserve the already truncated 2. Our money columns use numeric(10,2), and SUM returns numeric. Check a doubtful expression with PostgreSQL’s <code>pg_typeof(expression)</code> as well as its displayed value.</p>
 <p><code>COALESCE(value, fallback)</code> returns the first non-null argument. We will use it to make an absent payment subtotal zero, because the payment amounts themselves cannot be null. We will leave an absent line subtotal unknown, because the report cannot infer an order’s price from no lines. A convenient default needs a reason.</p>
 <p><code>timestamptz</code> is PostgreSQL’s short name for timestamp with time zone. It represents an instant and displays it in the session’s time zone; it does not retain the original zone name. The setup sets the session to UTC, and the query’s typed TIMESTAMPTZ literals carry explicit +00 offsets. Its half-open period includes the first instant of January and excludes the first of February. An upper bound of “31 January 23:59:59” would miss later fractional seconds.</p>
 <p>A local business day needs boundaries in its intended named zone before comparison with stored instants; it need not last 24 hours when clocks change. <a href="/review/relational-next/sql-values/">Getting SQL values right</a> develops that calculation, deliberate defaults, conversions and text comparison. Here the reporting clock is explicitly UTC.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="combine-related-facts-without-changing-the-question">
 <h2 id="combine-related-facts-without-changing-the-question">Combine related facts without changing the question</h2>
 <p>A <strong>join</strong> combines rows that satisfy a relationship condition. In the next query, o names an order row and l names a line row; they are table aliases. <code>ON l.order_id = o.order_id</code> asks for matching identities.</p>
 <Sql code={pairs} label="One result row per matching order and line" />
 <p>Predict how often 101 appears. Then replay Figure 1, and change the inner join to a left join. Watch what happens to order 106.</p>
 <SqlFigure scene="join" number={1} />
 <p>The inner join returns <code>(101, 1, 20)</code>, <code>(101, 2, 10)</code> and <code>(102, 1, 20)</code>. Order 101 appears twice because it has two matching lines. Order 106 has none, so it disappears. Replacing JOIN with <code>LEFT JOIN</code> preserves each unmatched order with NULLs for the line columns, adding <code>(106, NULL, NULL)</code>.</p>
 <p>An <strong>aggregate</strong> calculates over several input rows. <code>GROUP BY</code> gathers rows under their grouping values and emits one row per group. To recover one row per order, group by order_id and sum each order’s line amounts:</p>
 <Sql code={grouped} label="One row per order, preserving an unknown total for no lines" />
 <p>The totals by ID are <code>100 → 10</code>, <code>101 → 30</code>, <code>102 → 20</code>, <code>103 → 40</code>, <code>104 → 50</code>, <code>105 → 60</code> and <code>106 → NULL</code>. SUM ignores null inputs and returns NULL with no known amounts. COUNT(*) counts rows, while COUNT(column) counts non-null values: the left join’s one preserved row for 106 would count as one joined row but zero real line numbers.</p>
 <p><code>WHERE</code> filters the input rows; <code>HAVING</code> filters completed groups. To select orders whose line total is at least 30, filter on the sum:</p>
 <Sql code={having} label="Keep groups by a property of the whole group" />
 <p>Adding payments directly to the line join is a trap. Order 101 has two lines and two payments. Each line matches each payment, producing four rows: (20,10), (20,5), (10,10) and (10,5). Summing those rows gives line value 60 and payments 30, twice both correct totals. The balance 30 is plausible and wrong.</p>
 <details><summary>The wrong query, to test against the hand calculation</summary><Sql code={fanout} label="Wrong: joining both detail sets multiplies each contribution" /></details>
 <p>Calculate each detail total separately, then join one total per order from each side. The next section does that. <a href="/review/relational-next/joins-and-aggregation/">Joining and summarising data</a> develops the cardinality, outer-join filters and completeness decisions behind larger reports. Its separate exercise relates payments to customers; do not silently substitute that schema for this order-settlement fixture.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="checkable-stages">
 <h2 id="checkable-stages">Express a larger question in checkable stages</h2>
 <p>A <strong>common table expression</strong>, or CTE, names a query result for use within one statement. <code>WITH line_totals AS (...)</code> gives the parenthesised SELECT a name. The following SELECT reads that named result as it would read a table:</p>
 <Sql code={smallWith} label="Name one checked stage, then inspect its rows" />
 <p>The stage has one row per order with at least one line. It returns 100:10, 101:30, 102:20, 103:40, 104:50 and 105:60. There is no row for 106. An output column’s alias becomes the column name available to the next query, so line_totals exposes order_id and order_value. This name lasts only until the statement’s semicolon; it does not create a stored table.</p>
 <p>For several stages, write WITH once and separate the definitions with commas. A later stage can read an earlier one. Here line_totals and payment_totals each produce at most one row per order, so joining either to orders cannot multiply an order row. balances subtracts the two amounts and selects the January candidates:</p>
 <p>Step through Figure 2 before reading the complete statement. Follow order 101’s line and receipt identities into the independent 30 and 15 subtotals, then their difference. At the balances stage, compare 102’s absent payments with 106’s absent lines. Change one input at the same stage to see which values change, then advance to the final filter.</p>
 <ReportFigure />
 <Sql code={report} label="The complete follow-up report, ready to run after setup" />
 <p>The final SELECT keeps positive or unknown balances, adds the customer’s name and orders the answer. Its <code>EXISTS (...)</code> expression is a presence test: true if the subquery finds any payment row for that order, false otherwise. <code>receipt.order_id = o.order_id</code> connects the inner question to the current outer order; that is correlation. SELECT 1 supplies an arbitrary value because only the existence of a row matters. Two payments still give one Boolean, so the test does not multiply rows.</p>
 <p>To inspect an intermediate result, keep its WITH definitions and replace the final SELECT. For example, end with <code>SELECT * FROM balances ORDER BY placed_at, order_id;</code>. Expect the five candidates to have balances <code>100 → 0</code>, <code>101 → 15</code>, <code>102 → 20</code>, <code>106 → NULL</code>, <code>103 → 0</code>. Then restore the final SELECT. If the final answer is wrong, find the first stage whose rows break its own contract.</p>
 <p>These names organise reasoning. They do not promise that the engine stores each stage or executes the statement in this written sequence. That decision belongs to the query plan.</p>
 <p>Other questions need other composition. UNION combines rows from two compatible results, removing duplicate rows unless UNION ALL is requested; INTERSECT finds shared rows and EXCEPT finds rows present only on the left. Those operate on complete result rows. A category tree needs repeated parent-to-child expansion, which a recursive CTE can express. Recursion is optional for traversal tasks; none of this report, pagination or change workflow needs it. <a href="/review/relational-next/query-composition/">Composing larger SQL queries</a> develops membership, scalar results, reusable views and an optional traversal with explicit termination and cycle rules.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="compare-rows-while-keeping-their-detail">
 <h2 id="compare-rows-while-keeping-their-detail">Compare rows while keeping their detail</h2>
 <p>Grouping Mina’s orders by customer would replace them with one customer row. Suppose the screen needs each order, with the customer’s combined order value beside it. A <strong>window calculation</strong> computes across related rows while retaining each input row:</p>
 <Sql code={windows} label="Keep three order rows and attach their customer totals" />
 <p>The output is <code>(101, 1, 30, 50)</code>, <code>(102, 1, 20, 50)</code> and <code>(103, 2, 40, 40)</code>. OVER makes SUM a window calculation. PARTITION BY customer_id divides the three input rows by customer, and without a window ordering this SUM uses every row in its partition. The 50 is repeated beside Mina’s orders on purpose; summing that repeated column later would count her total twice.</p>
 <p>The WHERE condition defines the available rows before the window calculation, so the 50 is for Mina’s selected orders 101 and 102, not every order she ever placed. The final ORDER BY controls display. It does not tell the calculation which rows to use.</p>
 <p>An ordering inside OVER serves a different purpose. <code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY placed_at, order_id)</code> numbers each customer’s selected orders in that order. An ordered SUM can produce running totals, but its frame decides which preceding rows and tied peers it includes. <a href="/review/relational-next/window-functions/">Calculating across related rows</a> develops ranking, ties, running and moving calculations without losing the required detail.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="order-and-page-a-result-deliberately">
 <h2 id="order-and-page-a-result-deliberately">Order and page a result deliberately</h2>
 <p>A query without ORDER BY promises no row order. A timestamp alone also leaves ties: orders 101 and 102 share an instant. Use the unique order_id as a tie-breaker. PostgreSQL supports <code>NULLS FIRST</code> and <code>NULLS LAST</code> for nullable sort expressions; for example, <code>ORDER BY shipped_at ASC NULLS LAST, order_id ASC</code> places waiting orders last. Defaults and supported spellings differ between engines, so include the intended missing-value placement rather than inferring it from one sample.</p>
 <p>Take a separate list of all orders, in ascending placement order. PostgreSQL’s LIMIT returns the first two. The SQL-standard spelling <code>FETCH FIRST 2 ROWS ONLY</code> also works here.</p>
 <Sql code={firstPage} label="First page: 100 and 101, with 101’s timestamp and ID as the cursor" />
 <p>The fixed list is <code>100, 101, 102, 105, 106, 103, 104</code>. An offset page adds <code>OFFSET 2</code> to skip two rows and LIMIT 2 to take the next two, initially 102 and 105. <strong>Keyset pagination</strong> instead asks for rows strictly after the last returned key. Because 101 and 102 tie on time, both timestamp and ID belong in the cursor:</p>
 <Sql code={nextPage} label="Next page after 101: 102 and 105" />
 <p>The predicate says “a later timestamp, or the same timestamp with a greater ID”. PostgreSQL can shorten it to <code>(placed_at, order_id) &gt; (TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-01-10 09:00:00+00', 101)</code>; the expanded form makes the tie rule visible. A descending traversal reverses both its sort direction and comparison. Nullable cursor fields need a matching explicit missing-value policy; this fixture’s cursor fields are non-null.</p>
 <p>In Figure 3, choose an insertion between requests. Compare Offset 2 with After cursor, then try deleting an earlier row. Finally move order 102’s timestamp before the cursor.</p>
 <SqlFigure scene="paging" number={3} />
 <p>Inserting 107 before 101 shifts row positions: OFFSET 2 repeats 101, while the keyset request still returns 102 and 105. Deleting 100 makes OFFSET 2 skip unread 102; the cursor still starts after 101. Neither traversal picks up a newly inserted row before a cursor that has already passed it.</p>
 <p>Changing 102’s timestamp to before the saved cursor makes the keyset traversal miss it too. A previously read row moved after the cursor could reappear. A cursor names a boundary in an ordering, not a frozen database. Decide whether the application wants a live feed or a fixed export. <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Making concurrent changes correct</a> explains snapshot guarantees; <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Understanding and improving database performance</a> explains why skipping many rows can be expensive and which access path serves the chosen order.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="change-the-intended-rows-and-inspect-the-outcome">
 <h2 id="change-the-intended-rows-and-inspect-the-outcome">Change the intended rows and inspect the outcome</h2>
 <p>A change needs its own contract: the target identities, the precondition under which it is allowed, and the state it must leave. Suppose we may cancel order 101 only while it is open. SELECT its key and status first for inspection, then put that precondition in the UPDATE itself:</p>
 <Sql code={changes} label="Inspect an update, insert and delete, then roll back the exercise" />
 <p>UPDATE names the table, SET supplies new values, and WHERE selects the target rows. The first UPDATE returns <code>(101, cancelled)</code> and psql reports UPDATE 1. The second matches zero rows because the status has changed; it returns no row and reports UPDATE 0. Zero matches may mean absent identity or a failed precondition, so do not turn it into an unconditional success message.</p>
 <p>INSERT explicitly names its columns. Omitted shipped_at becomes NULL under this table definition. DELETE removes the selected row; the temporary order 107 has no lines or payments that would prevent its removal through the foreign keys. PostgreSQL’s RETURNING clause supplies rows directly affected by each statement: the inserted or updated values, or the deleted row’s values. This syntax and affected-row reporting vary by engine and driver.</p>
 <p>Before rollback, the inspection finds 101 cancelled and no 107. BEGIN groups the exercise into a transaction; ROLLBACK discards its database changes. The last SELECT finds 101 open again and no 107. For a deliberate kept change, COMMIT ends the transaction instead. Finish these exercises promptly rather than leaving a transaction open while reading.</p>
 <p>Omitting WHERE from UPDATE or DELETE selects every row. A WHERE on customer_id can also select several orders, quite correctly for a batch but incorrectly for a single-order action. Inspect identities and returned values against the intended postcondition, not just a positive row count. Use <a href="/review/relational-next/changing-data/">Changing data with SQL</a> for set-based changes, constraints, generated identifiers and insert conflicts. A prior SELECT is useful inspection but does not protect a later change from other writers; the transaction branch develops that separate correctness question.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="check-the-answer-before-trusting-the-query">
 <h2 id="check-the-answer-before-trusting-the-query">Check the answer before trusting the query</h2>
 <p>Run setup.sql, run the complete report, then compare it with the expectation. PostgreSQL returned these three rows, in this order. The amounts have numeric type; has_payment has Boolean type.</p>
 <table><thead><tr><th>order_id</th><th>name</th><th>due</th><th>has_payment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>101</td><td>Mina</td><td>15.00</td><td>true</td></tr><tr><td>102</td><td>Mina</td><td>20.00</td><td>false</td></tr><tr><td>106</td><td>Edda</td><td>NULL</td><td>false</td></tr></tbody></table>
 <p>A repeatable check should compare values, types and row multiplicities. Check the original output types separately with pg_typeof, as above: set operations can convert compatible differing types before comparing values. The following check compares values and row multiplicities; it returns no rows when the actual and expected rows agree. EXCEPT ALL subtracts matching row copies; checking both directions detects missing, extra or changed rows, including accidental repeats. It deliberately does not compare order, which we check separately from the ordered report above.</p>
 <details><summary>Executable comparison of actual and expected rows</summary><Sql code={checks} label="No differences in either direction is the expected result" /></details>
 <p>The fixture earns its space by separating failure cases. Orders 101 and 102 have equal placement times and repeated line amounts across orders; their distinct identities must survive. Order 101 has multiple lines and multiple payments, 102 lacks payments, and 106 lacks lines. shipped_at is null on 101 and present on 102. Orders 100 and 104 sit exactly on the two period boundaries; 103 tests the last second of January. The cancelled order must remain excluded even though it has a value.</p>
 <p>Check independent facts too: line_totals must have six distinct order IDs, payment_totals three, and balances five. Within balances, four known order values total 100 and their receipts total 65, leaving known balances totalling 35 and one unknown balance. Calculate those input totals separately, without joining lines to payments. A correct final row count would not detect their multiplication.</p>
 <p>The verification also changed the data inside rolled-back transactions. Emptying all four tables gave no report rows. Removing payments left known balances 10, 30, 20 and 40 plus 106’s unknown balance. Adding another 10-unit line to 101 raised its due to 25; adding another 5-unit receipt reduced it to 10. These are separate fixtures, each reset to the original before the next change. An update by missing order ID matched zero rows, the conditional change of 101 matched one then zero, and a change by customer 1 matched all three intended orders.</p>
 <p>Choose additional fixtures for the question you actually have: null measured amounts need a completeness policy beyond this fixture’s non-null amounts; time-zone reports need their own daylight-saving boundaries. Children check the further traps they introduce. Keep a failing query beside a tiny input that exposes it, then rerun the corrected query and the original cases.</p>
 <p>When finished, rerun setup.sql to reset, or disconnect with <code>\q</code> and remove the disposable database with PostgreSQL’s shell command <code>dropdb sql_working</code>, using the same connection details.</p>
 <details><summary>Remove the private practice server</summary>
  <p>If you started the private server above, use the same terminal and its saved directory. This waits for that server to stop before removing its disposable files. If shutdown fails, keep the directory and inspect the error.</p>
  <pre data-private-practice-stop><code is:raw>if pg_ctl -D "$SQL_PRACTICE_DIR/data" -m fast -w -t 15 stop; then
  rm -r -- "$SQL_PRACTICE_DIR"
fi</code></pre>
 </details>
 <p>For a first application, continue with <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Making concurrent changes correct</a> before choosing the deeper SQL articles below. It develops the protection a correct statement needs when other requests change the same facts. If your checked query is too slow, take it to <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Understanding and improving database performance</a> with representative data; correct answers alone do not establish an acceptable cost.</p>
</section>

